### AB

- **protocol baseline → change** (`base` → `post`): n=1 +42.3%, n=2 +45.4%,
  n=4 +37.1%, n=8 +0.6% (8/8 served against 7/8). This delta is *not* the
  scheduling change: `fpq-baseline` is main@7e3d2e6 and the change is
  merge/transport, whose reduce pushdown and segment work are most of the
  n≤4 gain. It does establish that the change is not below the old baseline
  anywhere, including N=8.
- **same tree, before → after** (`pre` → `post`): n=1 −2.9%, n=2 −2.2%,
  n=4 −5.0%, n=8 +1.3%. All inside this host's noise. Per-request savepoints
  cost nothing measurable; scheduling does not change a pinned-stream cell.
- **same image, eventfd vs default shm at N=8**: 34.93M against 54.26M,
  **−35.6%**, while serving 8 rings instead of 7. This is the gap the branch
  set out to close, and it is still there: one worker carries two rings and
  serialises their batch turns, so the pair finishes at ~2× the singleton wall.
  The six untasked workers go idle while it does.
- **the lever** (`post-16`, `max_worker_processes=16`): 55.71M at **8/8**, +1.3%
  on the same image's 7-worker shm (54.26M) and +59.5% on the sharing cell.
  With a slot per ring the engine itself is at parity; every point of the N=8
  deficit is the missing eighth backend.
- **the 1:1 shape on the new engine** (`post-rpw1`): 53.60M at 7/8, which
  reproduces the default path's 54.26M. The pool is not slower per ring; it is
  the second ring on the seventh worker that is.

### Verdict

**Negative on the throughput goal.** Scheduling cannot make eight long streams
on seven single-threaded backends run faster than seven streams on seven
backends. A stream is an SPI portal inside one worker's transaction; its batches
must run on that worker's OS thread, and no dispatch order moves the executor
turn to a worker that is idle (there is none while all eight read). The only
measures that close the N=8 gap are more worker slots — which is exactly
`max_worker_processes`, the control above — or splitting one stream across
workers, which the portal model does not do.

**What the branch does deliver, measured:**

- per-request **savepoints**, so a PG error on one ring no longer aborts the
  worker's transaction and every other stream it serves; the failing ring loses
  only its own stream (`python/tests/test_eventfd.py` finds a worker carrying
  two rings, fails one stream, and proves its neighbour's cursor still fetches);
- **idle-aware overflow**: registry v2 publishes a `busy` hint and
  `last_service_ms`, and the attach policy prefers a quiet worker over one in
  the middle of a read (useful when many rings are mostly idle, the pool's real
  deployment shape; it cannot help the all-busy cell);
- **round-robin dispatch**: each worker serves every ready ring once per turn,
  starting after the last one served, so the lowest index no longer owns the
  head of the loop; one batch is the time slice.
- N≤4 is neutral within noise, and N=8 keeps 8/8 service at the old baseline
  image's throughput. If the eighth reader matters more than ~36%, raise
  `max_worker_processes` (or accept the 7/8 fallback); that is a deployment
  choice, not a scheduler bug.

